data: {"type":"phase-complete","phase":"investigate","summary":"I'll run parallel investigations — pulling institutional runbook knowledge on Stripe timeout patterns AND confirming the vendor status in real time simultaneously.All four signals are back. The picture is crystal clear — here's the full diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n## 🔴 INC-001 Root Cause Diagnosis\n\n### Root Cause\n**Stripe's Payment Intents / `charge.create` API entered a degraded state at `14:18Z` (confirmed via Stripe status page), causing our `checkout-api` to accumulate latency (2840ms–2901ms slow calls) and timeouts. By `14:22:45Z`, our circuit breaker tripped OPEN — but because downstream callers have no graceful degradation path, every subsequent checkout request is now failing hard with a 503 (5012ms+ response) rather than falling back, converting Stripe's upstream partial failure into a full checkout outage.**\n\n---\n\n### Evidence Chain\n\n- 🔴 **`fetchVendorStatus` (Stripe):** Confirmed `\"degraded performance — Payment Intents API elevated error rate since 14:18Z\"` — 3 minutes *before* customer complaints at 14:21Z. This is the originating event.\n- 🟡 **`queryLogs` (checkout-api, warn/error):** Exact failure sequence logged: `stripe.charge.create slow_call` at 14:21:02 (2840ms) → `timeout after 5000ms` at 14:21:33 → `Circuit breaker OPEN for stripe.charge` at 14:22:45 → `POST /v1/checkout/complete 503 5012ms — circuit open` at 14:23:00. Precise 2-minute escalation chain.\n- 📘 **`searchRunbook` (RB-101 — \"Upstream payment provider slowdown / circuit breaker tripping\"):** Exact pattern matched with score=1. Institutional diagnosis: *\"Stripe upstream latency increase causes our P99 spike. Circuit breaker tripped open as designed, but caller services don't have graceful degradation.\"* — confirms this is a known failure mode, not a novel event.\n\n---\n\n### Confidence: **HIGH** ✅\nAll three independent signal sources (external vendor status, internal error logs with timestamps, internal runbook) converge on the same failure chain with no contradictions.\n\n---\n\n### Blast Radius\n| Scope | Impact |\n|---|---|\n| **User-facing** | 100% of new checkout completions failing (503) while circuit is OPEN |\n| **Revenue** | All card-based order completions blocked; no payment capture possible |\n| **Retry risk** | Without idempotency controls, background retries may double-charge on Stripe recovery |\n| **Unaffected** | Cart, product, auth, and order-read services — this is isolated to the `stripe.charge.create` call path |\n\n---\n\n### Top 3 Recommended Actions\n\n**1. 🚀 Enable Async-Queue Fallback for `charge.create` (RB-101 Step 3)**\n> Accept the order, enqueue the charge, retry when Stripe recovers — surface a \"payment pending\" UX state to users instead of a hard error.\n> - **Risk/Side-effect:** Requires idempotency key enforcement on retry to prevent double-charges. Do NOT enable without validating `stripe_idempotency_key` is set per request. Also increases order fulfillment latency until queue drains on Stripe recovery.\n\n**2. 🔧 Tune Circuit Breaker for Faster Half-Open Recovery**\n> Current config: `threshold=50% errors over 30s window` (per RB-101). Reduce half-open probe interval to 15s so that the breaker can re-test Stripe connectivity quickly once they mitigate, rather than leaving checkout dead for the full window.\n> - **Risk/Side-effect:** A shorter probe window risks re-opening the breaker too aggressively if Stripe's recovery is noisy/partial. Add a max-retry-in-half-open cap of 3 probes before re-opening.\n\n**3. 📟 Page Payments Team + Set Stripe Webhook for Recovery**\n> Per RB-101 Step 4: page the payments team now since Stripe status confirms outage is ongoing past the 15-minute SLA threshold (started 14:18Z, now 14:23Z+). Additionally, subscribe to Stripe's status webhook so automated recovery flows (drain queue, reset breaker) can trigger on Stripe's `resolved` event without human polling.\n> - **Risk/Side-effect:** Webhook handler must be idempotent — a duplicate `resolved` event should not trigger a double-drain. Validate handler logic before enabling in prod.\n\n---\n\n> **Immediate next owner action:** Payments team lead to confirm async-queue is safe to enable (idempotency key audit), then cut a fast-follow to wire in the Stripe status webhook for self-healing on recovery."}

